-E»‡^Œ.· ‰èü‰>ÅnÚˆÅVˆ¶þº«Ñ88+}{"url":"/gcsl/tools/editor-integration/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/tools/editor-integration/","content":"Configuring an Editor. The GCSL language server supplies diagnostics and navigation from the same checker that runs programs. You can use it in an editor that supports the Language Server Protocol (LSP) over standard input and standard output. Before you start # You need the following: An editor with configurable LSP support. The gcsl executable on the editor's command search path. A GCSL file or workspace to open. Connect the server # Associate files ending in .gcsl with a GCSL language entry in your editor. Set the server command to gcsl and its argument list to [\"lsp\"]. Use standard input and standard output for transport. Open a GCSL file. For a multifile project, open the workspace directory so the editor can supply file changes and watch notifications. Check the result # Hover over a declared name to see its type. Introduce a misspelled name and confirm that the editor displays a diagnostic. When an unambiguous spelling suggestion is available, a quick fix offers the replacement. The server checks source but does not evaluate programs. It supports completion, signature help, inlay hints, formatting, symbol search, and references. Rename checks the proposed edits before returning them and refuses changes that introduce errors or change name resolution. It does not rename files, directories, or standard-library declarations. Incomplete source # The index retains useful information around many syntax errors. For an unbalanced block, a query can use a repaired copy to answer navigation or completion while diagnostics still describe the real text. Rename needs the real source to be valid enough to verify and can be refused instead. Editor syntax highlighting is a separate integration. The grammar and queries in tree-sitter-gcsl are maintained in their own repository; they are not required for the gcsl lsp 